    "s02": {
      "type": "ms-statement",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "An architect's question",
        "use_content": true,
        "heading": "A sofa gets specified. A kitchen gets designed. The bed, where we spend a third of our lives, deserves the same care.",
        "text": "<p>Midsummer Milano began with a question its founder, an architect, kept asking: why is the bed treated as a technical product from a catalogue, when every other piece of a room is chosen with care?</p><p>Sleep Culture is where we follow that question: how material, temperature, proportion and ritual shape the night, and what making beds by hand, and listening to the people who sleep in them, has taught us.</p>"
      }
    },
    "s03": {
      "type": "ms-process",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "What a good night is made of",
        "heading": "Three things we design for.",
        "ground": "#F4EFE5"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "step_01": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_fallback": "ms-photo-linen-sheets.jpg",
            "title": "Temperature",
            "text": "<p>Natural fibres breathe. Wool and horsehair carry warmth and moisture away from the body, so the bed stays even through the night, in August as in January.</p>"
          }
        },
        "step_02": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_fallback": "ms-graded-ochre-room.jpg",
            "title": "Support",
            "text": "<p>Springs that work one by one follow the body instead of pushing back against it, at every point and in every position you sleep in.</p>"
          }
        },
        "step_03": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_fallback": "ms-photo-cream-blanket.jpg",
            "title": "The seasons",
            "text": "<p>Rest has a rhythm. Our toppers turn with the year: linen and silk to face the summer, wool and cashmere for the winter, in the same bed.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "step_01",
